WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:04.180
When you reach a certain level of wealth,
you start to feel like nothing can touch you.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.049
You spit sunshine and crap gold.

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:07.859
At least, that’s been my experience.

00:00:07.859 --> 00:00:10.049
Can’t imagine any of you can relate.

00:00:10.049 --> 00:00:14.360
But please, allow me to explain how it feels
when there’s three commas in your bank balance.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.870
I know time is money, but don’t worry, I’m
good for it.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.870
Or at least, I thought I was, before that
night...

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:24.730
Point is, the more money you get, the higher
you climb that golden ladder, the stronger

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.320
the feeling of security gets.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.620
Money can’t buy happiness, or so the saying
goes, but trust me when I say that it can

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.070
buy everything else.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.030
And that’s pretty close.

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:40.239
That’s right, I’m one of the dreaded one
percent: a straight-up billionaire, baby.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:44.010
Don’t get me wrong, I’m not some guy who
was born with a silver spoon in my mouth.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.149
I worked hard!

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:48.600
I was lucky, of course, and I had some help
from my parents, but who didn’t get a little

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.760
help from their families while growing up?

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.260
After my dad helped me get into an Ivy League
school, and gave me a 20 million dollar loan

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.960
as a graduation present, I was completely
on my own.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.149
I scraped, and I saved, and I turned that
loan into the biggest tech conglomerate in

00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:02.750
the entire world.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.959
After I made my first hundred million, I stopped
worrying about being successful enough, driven

00:01:06.959 --> 00:01:08.549
enough, good enough.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:09.649
After my first billion?

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:11.679
I felt like I could do anything.

00:01:11.679 --> 00:01:13.350
And honestly, I could.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.889
The money stopped feeling real, stopped being
anything except a number ticking ever upward,

00:01:17.889 --> 00:01:20.750
and signifying that I would never run out
of points in the game of life.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:21.750
I had won.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.710
I purchased myself a rocket with a velvet
interior, for luxury space travel.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.770
I filled a room in my house with vintage guitars
that had all previously been touched by members

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:31.729
of The Beatles, just because I could.

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:36.539
I moved into a cottage in Tuscany, a house
in Martha’s Vineyard, a beach house in Hawaii,

00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:41.259
my Los Angeles mansion, and my favorite, a
villa in the South of France.

00:01:41.259 --> 00:01:44.259
That’s not all of them, of course, but those
are my top five.

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:49.189
If you ever get the chance to have a house
on every continent, I highly recommend it.

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:51.740
Just kidding, I know most people won’t get
the chance.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.350
But maybe if you’re lucky, you’ll get
an invitation to one of my legendary parties.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:00.639
If you’re luckier than lucky, you’ll find
yourself with an invitation to my annual costume

00:02:00.639 --> 00:02:01.639
party.

00:02:01.639 --> 00:02:05.659
It's an incredible spectacle every year, and
this year was no exception.

00:02:05.659 --> 00:02:10.950
Picture it: me, my beautiful wife - an international
supermodel, thank you very much - and a few

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:15.200
hundred of our closest friends gathered at
my home nestled right up against the French

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.200
Riviera.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.010
The theme was “masquerade ball”, and the
dress code was strict.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:24.040
No one would be admitted without a mask or
attire elegant enough for the party.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.610
Inside, what greeted them was nothing short
of absolute opulence.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:31.000
I instructed my party planner to spare no
expense.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.470
The ballroom was outfitted with a massive
Swarovski chandelier, thousands of candles

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:40.420
lighting the room, flames sparkling in the
crystal, and filling the room with warm light.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.420
In the corner, a string quartet borrowed from
the National Opera of Paris played next to

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.570
one of six - count them, six - champagne fountains.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:52.650
I commissioned a portrait of me and my wife,
dressed to the nines and wearing our masks,

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:55.470
and hung it in the entryway to greet my guests.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:03:00.000
In the dining room, guests could sit down
to a feast fit for Louis XIV, complete with

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.450
30 courses including pheasant, pate, poached
pears, whole roasted boar, and other dishes

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.680
inspired by the Palace of Versailles.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.850
For party goers with more modern palates,
the molecular gastronomy room was open with

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:15.560
a chef on-site to serve flavored foams and
vaporized cocktails.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.560
Or, those who wanted to stick to the ballroom
could enjoy the passed-around hors d'oeuvres.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.060
But really, why choose?

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.720
If they wanted, they could have a bit of everything.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.450
Or a lot of it.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.400
Money was truly no object.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.070
The party was going great.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.780
I was wearing my favorite suit, which cost
more than most people spend on a house, and

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.350
a custom-designed mask made from hundreds
of black and white diamonds, with a few red

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:41.550
diamonds thrown in for a pop of color - and
because they’re the rarest variety of diamond

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:42.550
in the world.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:43.760
The more you know, right?

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.050
My guests had all arrived, and guards were
stationed at all entrances to make sure no

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:53.980
party crashers tried to make their way inside
and take advantage of my hospitality and generosity.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:59.910
So, imagine my surprise when, long after the
guest list had been completely checked off,

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:03.920
a mysterious figure swept in and interrupted
everything.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.850
My wife elbowed me in the side as I was helping
myself to some beluga caviar.

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:09.080
“Who is that?”

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.990
she hissed to me, tilting her head toward
the doorway at the other end of the ballroom.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:17.579
I turned, expecting to see a foreign dignitary
or oil baron she had forgotten the name of,

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:20.209
but instead, I saw something much more bizarre.

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:24.939
There, standing like a shadow in a room full
of silk and gold, was a person dressed in

00:04:24.939 --> 00:04:26.110
thick black robes.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:29.939
They were wearing a mask, but it was unsettling
to look at.

00:04:29.939 --> 00:04:34.990
Intense dark eyes peered out of pale ceramic
that tapered into a long, pointed beak.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:39.419
I dimly remembered drawings of Medieval doctors
wearing masks like those as the Black Plague

00:04:39.419 --> 00:04:40.849
decimated Europe.

00:04:40.849 --> 00:04:45.889
This was just some edgy party crasher, looking
for attention, I knew that, but looking at

00:04:45.889 --> 00:04:47.860
them…a chill ran up my spine.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:49.509
“How did he get in?”

00:04:49.509 --> 00:04:53.189
I shook my head, motioning to one of the security
guards stationed near the entrance.

00:04:53.189 --> 00:04:54.569
Do your jobs, fellas.

00:04:54.569 --> 00:04:55.569
Come on.

00:04:55.569 --> 00:04:58.380
Rob, my beefiest guard, got the signal and
approached the crasher.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.379
I took a sip of my drink, eager to watch this
wannabe cool guy get booted from my world.

00:05:03.379 --> 00:05:07.889
He watched Rob come closer as he stood completely
still, little black eyes unblinking.

00:05:07.889 --> 00:05:09.620
What was this guy’s deal?

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:13.680
He reached out one black-gloved hand and touched
Rob’s throat with one finger.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.020
As soon as the stranger touched his skin,
Rob dropped to the ground, unmoving.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:22.430
He was all the way on the other side of the
room, but somehow I just knew: he was dead.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:25.379
As soon as Rob fell, the party came to a screeching
halt.

00:05:25.379 --> 00:05:28.569
My wife screamed and tugged on my arm, but
I couldn’t take a step.

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:33.129
I just watched, frozen in place, as the stranger
in the Plague Doctor mask pulled a syringe

00:05:33.129 --> 00:05:37.449
from a pocket in his robes and injected something
thick and black into Rob’s corpse.

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:42.479
What had just been a mound of dead flesh began
to thrash around, hands grasping at nothing,

00:05:42.479 --> 00:05:47.900
legs kicking, and something that looked like
Rob, but was definitely not him, climbed to

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:48.900
its feet.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:52.710
It had his face, his body, but its eyes were
hollow and dead.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:56.009
There was no light behind them, they were
just flat.

00:05:56.009 --> 00:06:01.199
The thing that once was Rob began to shuffle
into the room, the stranger trailing behind

00:06:01.199 --> 00:06:02.199
it.

00:06:02.199 --> 00:06:04.599
As he walked, he touched guests with his gloved
hands.

00:06:04.599 --> 00:06:08.259
Like Rob, they dropped to the ground, dead
at a single touch.

00:06:08.259 --> 00:06:11.599
Guests began to scatter in every direction,
running for their lives.

00:06:11.599 --> 00:06:13.599
Still, I couldn’t get myself to move.

00:06:13.599 --> 00:06:15.819
A dozen people were dead on the floor.

00:06:15.819 --> 00:06:16.919
Then two dozen.

00:06:16.919 --> 00:06:17.919
Then three.

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:22.150
As he walked, the stranger injected them with
more of the strange liquid, and the dead guests

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:27.120
shambled to their feet or dragged themselves
along the floor with stiff, pale fingers.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.229
The shuffling corpses that were once my friends
grabbed guests near them with inhuman strength

00:06:32.229 --> 00:06:37.129
as they tried to run away, dragging them toward
the horrible Doctor so he could kill and transform

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:38.129
them too.

00:06:38.129 --> 00:06:42.210
I had to make a break for it, I had to get
out of there, but my legs wouldn’t listen

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:43.210
to me.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:46.279
Fear and morbid curiosity froze me to the
spot.

00:06:46.279 --> 00:06:50.229
There had to be at least fifty zombies by
now, and the Doctor just kept going.

00:06:50.229 --> 00:06:54.979
He was getting closer and closer to me, hand
outstretched and ready to deliver its deadly

00:06:54.979 --> 00:06:55.979
touch.

00:06:55.979 --> 00:06:59.009
I unstuck my tongue from the roof of my mouth,
swallowed, and spoke.

00:06:59.009 --> 00:07:01.059
“Why are you doing this?”

00:07:01.059 --> 00:07:04.689
The Doctor paused, tilting his head to the
side in thought.

00:07:04.689 --> 00:07:07.960
“This home is overrun with the Pestilence.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:12.089
It is my duty to contain its spread and treat
the infected.”

00:07:12.089 --> 00:07:17.169
He spoke formally, in a voice that reverberated
from inside the mask with a metallic edge.

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:19.499
“They must be cured.”

00:07:19.499 --> 00:07:20.909
I had to think fast.

00:07:20.909 --> 00:07:22.120
I could talk my way out of this.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.589
I’ve sold companies, made mergers, avoided
paying taxes my whole life.

00:07:25.589 --> 00:07:28.210
Now I just had to negotiate for my own life.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:31.330
My friends might be gone, but I could at least
save myself.

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:33.529
“You’re a Doctor,” I said.

00:07:33.529 --> 00:07:35.309
He seemed pleased at this.

00:07:35.309 --> 00:07:38.389
“I am a physician, yes,” he said.

00:07:38.389 --> 00:07:40.969
“You’re trying to help,” I continued.

00:07:40.969 --> 00:07:45.969
“Yes, I see you too are a man of science,”
He had stopped coming closer, the zombies

00:07:45.969 --> 00:07:47.409
had stopped advancing on me.

00:07:47.409 --> 00:07:48.409
It was working.

00:07:48.409 --> 00:07:52.550
“I don’t know if you’re aware of this,
but I’m a very wealthy man, Doctor,” I

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:56.860
took a drink of my champagne, doing my best
to appear casual, friendly even.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:59.740
“I’m sure you’re in need of funding,
for your mission?

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:01.659
Perhaps we could work together?”

00:08:01.659 --> 00:08:05.029
“You wish to aid me in my mission?”

00:08:05.029 --> 00:08:06.029
The Doctor asked.

00:08:06.029 --> 00:08:07.029
“I do,” I lied.

00:08:07.029 --> 00:08:12.560
“I’m in great health, as you can see,
but I don’t want a…pestilence spreading

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:14.759
around and infecting me or my loved ones.

00:08:14.759 --> 00:08:15.879
I’d like to help you.”

00:08:15.879 --> 00:08:18.219
I paused, searching for the right words.

00:08:18.219 --> 00:08:21.270
“I could be your Patron?”

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:25.039
The Doctor clapped his gloved hands together,
and I could have sworn he smiled beneath his

00:08:25.039 --> 00:08:26.240
beaked visage.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:27.240
“Excellent!

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:32.659
This is the most fortuitous meeting indeed,
I am glad to have found you here Sir.

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:35.680
If you would come with me, we may begin our
work!”

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.510
He turned and swept out of the room, gesturing
for me to follow.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:42.690
I looked around for an exit route, a way to
make a break for it, but there were too many

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:47.230
corpses ready to grab me with inhuman strength
and drag me to certain death.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:52.290
The room was filled with them, shuffling about
in their torn formal wear, slackened jaws

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:55.080
hanging loose and hollow eyes watching me.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:59.850
I had no choice but to follow this guy and
just wait for my opportunity to get away.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:04.480
I don’t know how long we walked down the
narrow town streets in the dark.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:08.040
Every time we passed an alley I thought I
could duck down, the Doctor got closer to

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.690
me with his deadly hands or a group of zombies
encircled me.

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:16.500
I was outside, I should have been home free,
but there was nowhere to run.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.210
Eventually, the Doctor stopped in front of
an abandoned, dilapidated house.

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:25.250
The paint was peeling off the walls, and there
was a hole that rotted through the ceiling.

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:27.750
“Welcome, Sir, to my Laboratory.”

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:31.330
He made a grand, sweeping gesture, inviting
me to walk through the door.

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:33.380
I obeyed, crossing the threshold.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:37.450
The smell of rot and dried blood hit me so
hard that I thought I might throw up.

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:42.360
The house was dark, I had to squint to see,
but I could make out scattered piles of body

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:43.950
parts, animal and human.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:49.450
There was a table covered with surgical equipment,
and, next to it, a black leather doctor’s

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:50.450
bag.

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:52.200
There were people in here, too.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.390
Or they used to be people.

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:57.420
They were silent zombies, like the others.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:01.400
Some stood along the walls at silent attention,
others were lying on the ground hooked up

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:06.930
to IVs full of strange liquid, or strapped
to gurneys ready to be operated on.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.330
“This looks great.”

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.790
My voice shook, but I hoped he wouldn’t
notice.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:15.980
“With my funds, we can upgrade your lab,
get you to a more high-tech facility.

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:18.370
Better lighting, more space, research assistants…”

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:19.370
“Excellent!

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:23.100
Together, we will eradicate the Pestilence
once and for all.”

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:26.020
“There” The Doctor pointed at the medical
table to my right.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:28.810
“Grab a pair of gloves and slide them on.”

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:32.970
The Doctor nodded, as I squeezed my hands
into a pair of rubber medical gloves.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:33.970
“Absolutely!

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:36.160
Can you show me more of what you’re working
on?”

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:37.160
Good thinking.

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:39.820
When his back was turned, I would run out
the door and get to safety.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:44.070
The Doctor approached the table covered with
tools, and bent to pick up a scalpel.

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.430
Now was my moment.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:48.450
I ran toward the door, as fast as my legs
would carry me.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:50.780
I collided with something hard and cold.

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:56.080
It was Rob, my old bodyguard, doing what he
did best: keeping the perimeter secure.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.080
“Sir?

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:59.180
Where are you going?”

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:00.250
The Doctor called.

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:03.440
“Are you…trying to abandon our work?”

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:07.160
He paused for a moment, realizing what I had
done.

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:10.280
“Did you deceive me?”

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:12.330
His voice turned cold, angry.

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:16.310
“No, I-“ I couldn’t come up with a lie.

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:20.640
Rob wrapped his arms around my chest, holding
me in place as the Doctor approached me with

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:21.830
the scalpel in hand.

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:27.270
“You are not looking well, my friend…Perhaps
you have fallen ill.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:31.400
Such a shame, I had looked forward to our
collaboration.”

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:34.930
He drew the scalpel, stretching his hand out
toward my neck.

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:38.440
I thrashed around, arms flailing, and grabbed
at his mask with gloved hands.

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:40.810
What did this monster look like underneath?

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:42.050
Who was he?

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:43.250
What was he?

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:47.940
The mask did not budge, and I realized…this
was no mask.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:49.490
It was his face.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:50.710
“Don’t worry.”

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:52.680
His blade sliced into the side of my neck.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:54.810
“I will make you well again.”

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:57.970
His pitch-black eyes twinkled as he set to
work.

00:11:57.970 --> 00:11:59.940
Then everything went dark...

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:06.180
Now go check out “SCP-049 - the Plague Doctor
Captured” and “SCP-049 - What Actually

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:07.270
is the Pestilence?

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:11.590
The Plague Doctor Questions and Theories”
for more stories of this not-so-good doctor!

